<%#
  The landing page. Copy comes from the approved design canvas (docs rework 2026) and is
  not edited here: wording changes go through the canvas and Henning's review.

  Code samples are plain code, highlighted by the `pre code` compiler like every other
  code block on the site. Its `mark:` and `chip:` comments mark an attribute or stand in
  for content.
%>
<% @frame = :landing %>
<%# The landing page has no Markdown twin. Agents get the generated index instead
    (root_index.txt.erb), which /llms.txt serves too. %>
<% head_link(rel: 'alternate', type: 'text/markdown', href: '/index.md') %>
<% head_link(rel: 'alternate', type: 'text/plain', href: '/llms.txt') %>

<div class="landing">

  <section class="landing--section">
    <div class="landing--inner">
      <h1 class="landing--claim">The missing application layer for&nbsp;HTML</h1>

      <p class="landing--byline">Unpoly expands what links, forms and server-rendered pages can do:</p>

      <div class="landing--annotated">
        <div class="landing--annotated-row">
          <pre class="landing--annotated-code"><code class="language-html">&lt;a href="/tasks/123" up-target="#pane"&gt;Details&lt;/a&gt; &lt;!-- mark: up-target --&gt;</code></pre>
          <span class="landing--annotated-lead" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="landing--annotated-note">updates a fragment</span>
        </div>
        <div class="landing--annotated-row">
          <pre class="landing--annotated-code"><code class="language-html">&lt;a href="/users/new" up-layer="new"&gt;Add user&lt;/a&gt; &lt;!-- mark: up-layer --&gt;</code></pre>
          <span class="landing--annotated-lead" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="landing--annotated-note">opens in a modal overlay</span>
        </div>
        <div class="landing--annotated-row">
          <pre class="landing--annotated-code"><code class="language-html">&lt;form up-validate&gt;…&lt;/form&gt; &lt;!-- mark: up-validate --&gt;</code></pre>
          <span class="landing--annotated-lead" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="landing--annotated-note">validates against your server</span>
        </div>
        <div class="landing--annotated-row">
          <pre class="landing--annotated-code"><code class="language-html">&lt;main up-poll&gt;…&lt;/main&gt; &lt;!-- mark: up-poll --&gt;</code></pre>
          <span class="landing--annotated-lead" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="landing--annotated-note">keeps itself fresh</span>
        </div>
        <span class="landing--annotated-more">… and many more.</span>
      </div>

      <p class="landing--trailer">
        The application stays on your server — any language, any framework.
        The browser learns just enough to feel like an app.
        Your HTML keeps its meaning — agents and crawlers can read your app without running JavaScript.
      </p>

      <div class="actions landing--actions">
        <a class="action -large" href="/learn">Learn Unpoly</a>
        <a class="action -large -secondary" href="/api">API Reference</a>
        <span class="landing--aside">or <%= hyperlink 'see it running in the demo', 'https://demo.unpoly.com', target: '_blank' %></span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="landing--section -tinted">
    <div class="landing--inner">
      <h2 class="landing--heading">Update only what changed</h2>

      <p>Following an Unpoly link doesn't tear down the page. The server responds with HTML —
         a full page is fine — and Unpoly extracts the targeted fragment, discarding the rest:</p>

      <%= partial 'fragment_updates_diagram' %>

      <p>The rest of the page stays: your scroll position, your focus,
         the form you were halfway through.</p>

      <p>And because every response can be a full page, every URL keeps working on its own —
         in a new tab, for a bookmark, for a crawler. You don't build separate fragment
         endpoints. Unpoly works with the routes you already have. Rendering only the fragment
         is a simple, optional optimization; most screens never need it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="landing--section">
    <div class="landing--inner">
      <h2 class="landing--heading">Edge cases included</h2>

      <p>Real applications are full of cases that demos skip: back buttons, racing requests,
         stale caches, users who double-click. Unpoly's defaults cover them.
         Here is a single enhanced link:</p>

<pre class="landing--code"><code class="language-html">&lt;a href="/projects" up-follow&gt;Projects&lt;/a&gt; &lt;!-- mark: up-follow --&gt;</code></pre>

      <p>When it's clicked, Unpoly:</p>

      <ul class="landing--checklist">
        <li>updates the main fragment and the browser URL</li>
        <li>preserves scroll positions, focus and half-typed input outside the fragment</li>
        <li>aborts an earlier request racing for the same fragment</li>
        <li>caches the response, and revalidates it when it goes stale</li>
        <li>restores the exact screen state when the user presses Back</li>
        <li>runs your component teardown before anything is swapped out</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The same care runs through every feature.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="landing--section -tinted">
    <div class="landing--inner">
      <h2 class="landing--heading">Built for tough requirements</h2>

      <p>These are the screens that usually get an app rewritten in React.
         With Unpoly they stay on your server — rendered as HTML, enhanced with a few attributes:</p>

      <div class="landing--cards">
        <div class="landing--card">
          <h3 class="landing--heading -minor">Reactive server forms</h3>
          <p>Fields that depend on other fields — selects loading their options, previews updating as you type —
             rendered by your server, wired with one attribute.</p>
          <%= hyperlink 'Learn more →', '/reactive-server-forms' %>
        </div>
        <div class="landing--card">
          <h3 class="landing--heading -minor">Optimistic rendering</h3>
          <p>Update the screen before the server answers, then reconcile with the real response.
             Previews revert cleanly when anything fails.</p>
          <%= hyperlink 'Learn more →', '/optimistic-rendering' %>
        </div>
        <div class="landing--card">
          <h3 class="landing--heading -minor">Complex overlay flows</h3>
          <p>Branch off into an overlay, complete a multi-page flow there, and return the result
             to the parent screen — which never went away.</p>
          <%= hyperlink 'Learn more →', '/subinteractions' %>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="landing--heading -sub">Escape hatches everywhere</h3>

      <p>When attributes aren't enough, you can recompose Unpoly's behavior using its
         extensive JavaScript API. Every attribute has a JavaScript twin —
         <code>[up-submit]</code> is <code>up.submit()</code>, modifying attributes become options.</p>

<pre class="landing--code"><code class="language-javascript">// Let the user create a record in an overlay, then use the result:
let { email } = await up.layer.ask({ url: '/users/new' })
up.submit('#invite-form', { params: { email } })</code></pre>

      <p>Along the way, Unpoly emits the <b>events</b> you wish the platform had:</p>

      <%# Each word links to its event, whose name shows as a tooltip. %>
      <div class="landing--event-groups">
        <% [
          ['link', 'Link', 'clicked' => 'up:click', 'following' => 'up:link:follow', 'preloading' => 'up:link:preload', 'lazy-loading' => 'up:deferred:load'],
          ['form', 'Form', 'submitting' => 'up:form:submit', 'validating' => 'up:form:validate', 'switching fields' => 'up:form:switch'],
          ['overlay', 'Overlay', 'opened' => 'up:layer:opened', 'accepted' => 'up:layer:accepted', 'dismissed' => 'up:layer:dismissed'],
          ['fragment', 'Fragment', 'loaded' => 'up:fragment:loaded', 'inserted' => 'up:fragment:inserted', 'keeping' => 'up:fragment:keep', 'destroyed' => 'up:fragment:destroyed', 'polling' => 'up:fragment:poll'],
          ['request', 'Request', 'slow' => 'up:network:late', 'aborted' => 'up:fragment:aborted', 'offline' => 'up:fragment:offline', 'recovered' => 'up:network:recover'],
          ['history', 'History', 'URL changed' => 'up:location:changed', 'going back' => 'up:location:restore'],
        ].each do |modifier, category, events| %>
          <span class="landing--event-category -<%= modifier %>"><%= category %></span>
          <div class="landing--event-tags">
            <% events.each do |label, event| %>
              <a href="/<%= event %>" data-event="<%= event %>" aria-label="<%= label %> (<%= event %>)"><%= label %></a>
            <% end %>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      </div>

      <p>And your own JavaScript plugs in through <b>compilers</b>. Here a Google Map gets its center from the server:</p>

      <div class="landing--pair">
<pre class="landing--code"><code class="language-html">&lt;div class="map" up-data="{lat: 50.9, lng: 7.4}"&gt;
  &lt;!-- chip: Map will appear here --&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
<pre class="landing--code"><code class="language-javascript">up.compiler('.map', (element, data) =&gt; {
  var map = new google.maps.Map(element)
  map.setCenter(data)
})</code></pre>
      </div>

      <p>The same pattern enhances existing tags, invents new elements, or mounts a whole React island.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="landing--section -wash">
    <div class="landing--inner">
      <h2 class="landing--heading">Your HTML keeps its meaning</h2>

      <p>Unpoly enhances HTML elements without changing what they are.
         A link still navigates and a form still submits — they just learn to do it better.</p>

      <blockquote class="landing--quote">
        <p>“A wonderful JavaScript library with best-in-class progressive enhancement.”</p>
        <cite>— Carson Gross, creator of <%= hyperlink 'htmx', 'https://htmx.org/', target: '_blank', class: '-gray' %></cite>
      </blockquote>

      <p>Everything that already understands HTML keeps understanding your app:
         search engines index the HTML your server sends, agents read your content without executing JavaScript,
         and screen readers find actual <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> elements — not clickable <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>s.
         And since your server can always answer with a complete page, every screen has
         its own URL — one you can share, bookmark or open directly.</p>

      <p><b>Every feature has a graceful degradation story.</b> For example, when we built lazy loading,
         we modeled it as a <em>self-loading hyperlink</em> — without JavaScript it's still a link,
         and the content is one click away instead of gone:</p>

<pre class="landing--code"><code class="language-html">&lt;a href="/menu" up-defer&gt;Load menu&lt;/a&gt; &lt;!-- mark: up-defer --&gt;</code></pre>

      <%# The same markup, two outcomes. %>
      <div class="landing--degrade">
        <figure class="landing--degrade-panel">
          <figcaption class="landing--caption -small">With JavaScript</figcaption>
          <ul class="landing--degrade-menu" aria-label="Example: the loaded menu">
            <li>Projects</li>
            <li>Tasks</li>
            <li>Reports</li>
          </ul>
        </figure>
        <figure class="landing--degrade-panel">
          <figcaption class="landing--caption -small">Without JavaScript</figcaption>
          <span class="landing--degrade-link">Load menu</span>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="landing--section -tinted">
    <div class="landing--inner">
      <h2 class="landing--heading">Is Unpoly right for you?</h2>

      <p>The hypermedia space has two philosophies. htmx states its own beautifully:</p>

      <blockquote class="landing--quote">
        <p>“Why should only <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> &amp; <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> be able to make HTTP requests?
           Why should only <code>click</code> &amp; <code>submit</code> events trigger them?”</p>
        <cite>— <%= hyperlink 'htmx.org', 'https://htmx.org/', target: '_blank', class: '-gray' %>, on its motivation</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <p>Unpoly makes the opposite bet: the elements HTML already gave us are the right ones to make requests —
         because the whole web already knows what they mean. So instead of generalizing requests to any element,
         Unpoly makes links and forms <em>more capable</em>, with strong defaults for the cases every app hits.</p>

      <p>Both approaches make sense. Small primitives give you maximum control and a minimal footprint.
         A higher-level layer gives you fewer attributes for common cases, with the edge cases already handled.</p>

      <div class="landing--fits">
        <div>
          <h3 class="landing--heading -minor">Unpoly is for you if…</h3>
          <ul class="bullets">
            <li>You render HTML on a server — Rails, Django, Laravel, Phoenix, Node, anything — and want to keep it that way.</li>
            <li>Your app has outgrown full page loads, but an SPA would mean duplicating your product in the browser.</li>
            <li>You want one mental model for navigation, overlays and forms — not a kit of separate libraries to combine.</li>
            <li>You want overlays, reactive forms and instant navigation without wiring the plumbing yourself.</li>
            <li>You expect to maintain this app for years.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="landing--heading -minor">Choose another tool when…</h3>
          <ul class="bullets">
            <li>Your application state truly belongs in the browser — an editor, a game, offline-first.</li>
            <li>You want maximum control over every request and DOM change — <%= hyperlink 'htmx', 'https://htmx.org', target: '_blank' %> is excellent for that temperament.</li>
            <li>You need native iOS/Android shells around your app and don't want to write your own — Hotwire ships ready-made adapters for that.</li>
            <li>A dedicated frontend team owns a separate client-side product.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <%# After when Unpoly is right for you, the companies it was right for. %>
      <div class="landing--logos">
        <p class="landing--caption">In production at</p>
        <div class="landing--logo-wall">
          <% {
            'vw' => 'Volkswagen', 'audi' => 'Audi', 'siemens' => 'Siemens', 'bosch' => 'Bosch', 'arm' => 'Arm',
            'zendesk' => 'Zendesk', 'steady' => 'Steady', 'urban-dictionary' => 'Urban Dictionary', 'tuifly' => 'TUIfly', 'adonisjs' => 'AdonisJS',
          }.each do |logo, name| %>
            <%= image_tag "logos/#{logo}.svg", class: 'landing--logo', alt: name %>
          <% end %>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="landing--section">
    <div class="landing--inner">
      <h2 class="landing--heading">Start with one screen</h2>

      <p>You don't need to migrate your application. Start where full page loads hurt most —
         a filter form that resets the page, an edit screen that should be an overlay, a slow report.
         Enhance one link. Leave the rest alone.</p>

      <p>Two lines, no build step:</p>

<pre class="landing--code"><code class="language-html">&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/unpoly@<%= guide.version %>/unpoly.min.css"&gt;
&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/unpoly@<%= guide.version %>/unpoly.min.js" defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>

      <div class="actions landing--actions">
        <a class="action -large -blue" href="/start/overview">Get started</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</div>
